              "reasoning": "**Considering AI contributions to math**\n\nLooking ahead to 2024, it seems like AI hasn't really produced groundbreaking new math yet. However, I’m forecasting that by 2025, we could see significant advancements in automated theorem proving. There are notable developments with systems like AlphaGeometry and OpenAI’s proof-checking. I wonder if, by 2024, events like the Hodge conjecture being solved could involve AI contributions. I think it’s important to establish probabilities that factor in this increasing capability of AI over time.**Estimating probabilities for Hodge conjecture**\n\nBy the end of 2026, I believe the probability of the Hodge conjecture being solved is really low, perhaps around 0.1 to 0.2%. If AI were to contribute, that chance might rise to about 10-30%. However, it's unlikely people will rely on AI for cutting-edge algebraic geometry by then, so I think a probability closer to 0.0002 is more realistic. Looking towards 2030, while the chance of the Hodge being solved still seems very small, there might be a 30-50% chance that AI will be involved in any solutions that arise.**Analyzing future probabilities for Hodge conjecture**\n\nBy 2030, I think mathematicians might start using AI as co-pilots, but the chance of a new argument from AI still seems low, maybe around 1%. If AI does contribute, that's about a 20% chance, leading to a combined probability of 0.2%. By 2035, I see that possibility rising to 3-5%, and if AI becomes stronger, the chance of AI's involvement could increase to 40%, giving us about 1.5-2%. As for 2040, while being optimistic is tempting, it’s a tough problem, so I estimate about 0.08. By 2050, that could rise to 20-35%, with AI contributing around 75-85%.**Weighing Hodge conjecture solutions**\n\nI need to think about how the Hodge conjecture might require new ideas. If AI can achieve automated theorem discovery beyond human capability by the 2040s, it seems possible that the Hodge could be solved by AI or with AI assistance by 2050. While some suggest it could be independent of ZFC, consensus leans toward it not being so. The definition of a \"solution\" might vary; just proving independence wouldn't count as a complete solution in the traditional sense, yet I wonder if Clay's view allows for independence to be considered a resolution.",
